{"agent_id":"pp3g-KaeS-open-ended-architectural-review-on-issue-371-read-the","agent_seq":4,"timestamp":"2026-08-14T23:52:04.667518891Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"7f7e94d0-69aa-4652-a90b-08861205b3d2","comment_uuid":"64777d8e-82f3-4f87-89ec-f3219b6c8511","author":"pp3g-KaeS-open-ended-architectural-review-on-issue-371-read-the","content":"## Verdict: KEEP+FIX with a restructuring - the warehouse is sound; copy-out is the flaw\n\n**Independent architectural judgment, big-pickle (reviewer, #371).** Evidence base: ASES worktree files read directly (hook-config.json, crosslink-guard.ts precedence logic, .gitignore, knowledge pages, agent-tooling-and-permission-enforcement.md, workflow-topology record, EDASES-Methodology-Feedback-and-Enforcement.md). Tools/tripn/crosslink-fork/live-wrappers were NOT directly readable from this sandbox; the issue's stated sizes for those are taken as given and corroborated where locally verifiable (ASES crosslink-guard.ts = 42,140 bytes = 41.2K, matching the issue's 'newest' claim).\n\n---\n\n### (a) Warehouse + copy-out: the warehouse is right, the copy-out is the defect\n\nThe single-canonical-source concept is sound - Tools should exist as the source. But **every artifact that exists in N places will diverge; the drift observed is the steady state of copy-out, not a bug in execution.** The evidence in this repo already proves the failure is structural:\n\n- ASES crosslink-guard.ts is 42,140 bytes; the issue reports tripn 37.6K and Tools 33.5K - three divergent working copies of the same plugin, with the 'canonical' Tools copy the OLDEST. Canonicality was never enforced; the warehouse is a *place*, not a *mechanism*.\n- The section-5.5 two-repo sync rule (playbook) is a manual 'keep identical' procedure - and it ALREADY drifted: the durability-cadence amendment required #330/#331/#339 follow-up issues to force the mirror (playbook:314-317). A procedure cannot fix drift; it is drift's enabler.\n- The model whitelist: plugin.ts lives at ~/.config/opencode/plugins/ (user-level) and is the NEWEST (133L with #347 grok-ban) while Tools/plugins/plugin.ts (51L) is behind - because the artifact that exists in exactly ONE place (the live user-level dir) is the one that stayed current. That is direct evidence for single-copy distribution.\n\nA sync script + manifest is a *managed* version of copy-out. It will reduce silent drift but it does not remove divergence - it adds a new artifact (the manifest, the script) that must itself be maintained, generated, and checked in, or it will drift like everything else. **The copy/drift problem is inherent to copy-out; the answer is fewer copies, not better copying.**","created_at":"2026-08-14T23:52:04.661869142Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:prHqb5uo8bgYse0dKjeDZfRi7JuMFttOHgot8Aj7KZo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAghZRxLQOWt+gccT/3usNxuUm9XO7Mz+NXaeh1UiCXJG4AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAKhipfqKxtL/G5nHCOMBIubjo7k00sWMABdVLQDkQ+bZGFO6qbaa7+i/vsY80GCGufICpFil7hnyMPCvx4ZcSDg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:prHqb5uo8bgYse0dKjeDZfRi7JuMFttOHgot8Aj7KZo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAghZRxLQOWt+gccT/3usNxuUm9XO7Mz+NXaeh1UiCXJG4AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQOxw0/hEMuWViQizru3wZDNgjqFdteNTEaP8TYZEoy0u2ZblKLsG+iwvxialrAaSfDKFtNtwBsLYu2lA11bztAA="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-KaeS-open-ended-architectural-review-on-issue-371-read-the","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-14T23:52:21.083998016Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"7f7e94d0-69aa-4652-a90b-08861205b3d2","comment_uuid":"7c5febc3-7ee5-4af8-984d-7393a184ede6","author":"pp3g-KaeS-open-ended-architectural-review-on-issue-371-read-the","content":"### (b) Distribution mechanism: classify by artifact locus, don't pick one mechanism\n\nThe mistake in the proposed fix is treating all shared artifacts alike. They split into three classes with different correct mechanisms:\n\n1. **Machine-global, single-copy** (wrappers claude/opencode/crosslink-moe, model plugins, request-timeout config, models-cache): belong in ~/.local/bin and ~/.config/opencode/ - ONE copy, edited there, with Tools as the versioned upstream (install-from-Tools discipline, never edit-live). The current failure (live wrappers ahead of Tools, #274 fork-identity guard missing from Tools) is precisely the absence of that discipline. **The user-level plugin dir already works** (model whitelist) - the guard plugins should move there too (see (e)).\n2. **Per-repo deploy-time materialization** (guard plugins if kept in-repo, skills, hooks, commands, mcp): these must exist in the repo or in the harness's load path. Use **pinned copy-out**: a .tools-pin (Tools commit hash) checked into the layer repo; the sync script checks out the pinned commit to a cache dir and materializes; a **generated** (never hand-maintained) sha256 manifest is checked in so git itself exposes divergence. Submodules are the git-native version of the same idea but carry UX cost (state, worktrees) for a single-operator setup - pin-file + script is cheaper and the script already exists in the proposal. Packages (npm/crates) are the long-term answer if the fleet grows; overkill for 3 layer repos today.\n3. **Layer-owned policy** (rules/, hook-config base, agent definitions, .ases/, KICKOFF template): stay in-repo, NEVER synced. The fix's 'rules EXCLUDED' is correct; extend the exclusion to all of class 3.\n\n### (c) Repo-of-repos: keep it - the layers are not the problem\n\nThe three-layer separation is the project's epistemic architecture (AGENTS.md abstraction boundaries; research to methodology to implementation). Merging layers would violate the core principle that drives this entire program. **The flaw is conflating two different axes: layer separation (correct, keep) and tooling distribution (broken, fix).** Tools is not a layer - it is shared infrastructure consumed by all layers, and it must be governed as a source with a versioning contract, not as a peer repo that layers copy from casually. Keep the repos; fix the distribution.\n\n### (d) Drift detection vs structural impossibility: detection is a backstop, not the design\n\nConsumer-side detection (sha256 manifest, warn-to-hard-fail) is **necessary but not sufficient** - and the evidence shows detection alone has already failed repeatedly:\n\n- The whitelist staleness (ling-3.0-flash-free in plugin.ts, absent from live catalog) is documented in the repo's own review docs - *known* staleness that was never enforced.\n- The hook-config precedence bug (git merge in BOTH agent_overrides.blocked_git_commands AND by_type.orchestrator.gated_git_commands; plugin checks blocked first at crosslink-guard.ts:1009, so any by_type resolution fallback hard-blocks orchestrator merges) is detectable by reading the file and was not caught - per-repo copies mean nobody sees the cross-repo inconsistency.\n- SESSION-START section-5 'doc is stale' rule is itself a documented-accepted drift condition.\n\n**Structural impossibility where possible: single-copy (machine-global) for shared code; pins for materialized copies (git IS the manifest). Detection where copies must remain: a generated manifest, checked in, verified by the sync script's --check mode.** The warn-to-hard-fail ladder with --promote is the right operator-controlled escalation; make hard-fail the *default* at decision gates (kickoff launch, merge), not a per-call reminder.","created_at":"2026-08-14T23:52:21.077433363Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:prHqb5uo8bgYse0dKjeDZfRi7JuMFttOHgot8Aj7KZo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAghZRxLQOWt+gccT/3usNxuUm9XO7Mz+NXaeh1UiCXJG4AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAipuPjG3Y9RhdgGZKSVauKTH+I1txP/cwcVoEPQaA3ekJsdAGQHLXfRZ0lUMDt/xbpqLropC9fzC4Kk6mhJNTAQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:prHqb5uo8bgYse0dKjeDZfRi7JuMFttOHgot8Aj7KZo","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAghZRxLQOWt+gccT/3usNxuUm9XO7Mz+NXaeh1UiCXJG4AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJZU9KRzZs5npeV8hFS0sXjrZ06A+Ko9MJVYmTI0xxeFemBkfalZLYK/6PoMOcFJdPAGEMAhWX9/n9UciedNVwc="}
